  "evidences": [
    {
      "id": "ev_1",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s1"
      },
      "text": "APT29 has used The Onion Router (TOR) and the TOR domain fronting plugin meek to create a hidden, encrypted network tunnel that appeared to connect to Google services over TLS.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_3",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s3"
      },
      "text": "We first discussed APT29’s use of these techniques as part of our “No Easy Breach” talk at DerbyCon 6.0.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_9",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s9"
      },
      "text": "First, it encodes TOR traffic into HTTP specifying the host name of the reflection server (for example, the default server meek-reflect.appspot.com).",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_15",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s15"
      },
      "text": "Note: Google suspended the reflection server meek-reflect.appspot.com, but other servers, in the Google cloud or other supported CDNs, can fulfill the same function.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_16",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s1"
      },
      "text": "Backdoor Overview Mandiant discovered that APT29 enabled a TOR hidden service that forwarded traffic from the TOR client to local ports 139, 445 and 3389 (NetBIOS, SMB and TS, respectively).",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_19",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s4"
      },
      "text": "C:\\Program Files(x86)\\Google\\googleService.exe C:\\Program Files(x86)\\Google\\GoogleUpdate.exe C:\\Program Files(x86)\\Google\\core",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_21",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p3_s1"
      },
      "text": "C:\\Program Files(x86)\\Google\\state C:\\Program Files(x86)\\Google\\start.ps1 C:\\Program Files(x86)\\Google\\install.bat The file googleService.exe is the primary TOR executable, responsible for establishing and maintaining encrypted proxy connections.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_24",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p3_s4"
      },
      "text": "Filename SHA256 The file C:\\Program Files (x86)\\Google\\core contains configuration information for the TOR service googleService.exe.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_25",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p3_s5"
      },
      "text": "Communicate on ports 1, 80 and 443 Bridge traffic using the meek plugin to https://meek-reflect.appspot.com and obfuscate HTTPS and DNS requests to appear destined for www.google.com",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_30",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s1"
      },
      "text": "The attackers used the scripts start.ps1 and install.bat to install the TOR service.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_33",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s4"
      },
      "text": "Installation and Persistence The attacker executed the PowerShell script C:\\Program Files(x86)\\Google\\start.ps1 to install the TOR services and implement the “Sticky Keys” exploit.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_35",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s6"
      },
      "text": "By replacing the “Sticky Keys” binary, C:\\Windows\\System32\\sethc.exe, with the Windows Command Processor cmd.exe, the attackers then accessed a privileged Windows console session without authenticating to the system.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_36",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s7"
      },
      "text": "Pressing the shift key five times activates “Sticky Keys” and executes sethc.exe, which, when replaced with cmd.exe, opens a System-level command shell.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_38",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s9"
      },
      "text": "The installation script start.ps1 created a Windows service named Google Update to maintain persistence after a system reboot.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_40",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "FireEye_APT29-Domain-Fronting-With-TOR(03-27-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 5,
        "section": "body",
